WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:15.410
Chile was once the most stable and prosperous
country in Latin America.

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:18.960
A role model.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.930
A kind of experiment to show that escaping
poverty isn’t an impossible goal, even in

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:26.400
Latin America.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:36.140
However, in October 2019, this model seemed
to begin to shatter.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.379
Suddenly waves of extreme violence and huge
political mobilizations erupted throughout

00:00:40.379 --> 00:00:41.379
the country.

00:00:41.379 --> 00:00:46.699
Within just a few weeks, the country was on
fire.

00:00:46.699 --> 00:00:50.789
Since the protests started, 26 people have
lost their lives; thousands have been wounded,

00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:55.989
more than 25,000 premises have been affected
by fires, looting or destruction, the Santiago

00:00:55.989 --> 00:01:00.639
metro has suffered damage worth hundreds of
millions of dollars and the economy has stopped.

00:01:00.639 --> 00:01:04.909
To give you an idea, according to government
data, the cost of these damages run between

00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:07.310
two and three billion dollars.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:13.500
Dear friends of VisualPolitik, in this video
we’ll tell you what happened in Chile, why

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:19.770
it happened and what lies ahead.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:29.380
Listen up.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:38.180
(THE SPARK THAT LIT THE FIRE)

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.890
The October social explosion was a surprise
that caught everyone off guard.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:47.580
Check this out, the turning point came when
the Chilean government decided to increase

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.860
the price of the Santiago metro ticket by
30 pesos, about $US 0.04.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.470
Yes, I know, it wasn't exactly a huge increase...

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:59.280
But let's say that was the reason, the motive,
the spark that lit the fire.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.580
Suddenly hundreds of students began to protest,
entering the subway without paying... but

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:04.580
it didn’t stop there.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:07.500
Little by little everything got out of hand.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:13.390
Look, we’re talking about Monday October
14th, well, by Saturday 19th the subway was

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:14.390
on fire:

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.530
Yes, that’s right.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:17.950
On fire.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:22.120
Half of the capital's subway stations had
suffered vandalism attacks and more than 20

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.800
were literally on fire.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:28.390
They were being reduced to ashes.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:33.600
To give you an idea, just the damages alone
from Saturday 19th cost an estimated $200

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:34.600
million.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:40.030
(AUDIO: Piñera announces a state of emergency
in Chile after an intense explosion of violence

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:41.670
due to the rise in subway rates.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:42.670
El Mundo)

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:43.670
But that was only the beginning.

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:52.670
In the following days, the situation got even
worse and protests spread to the rest of

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:53.670
the country.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:56.520
Suddenly, supermarkets, shops, buses, even
entire buildings were being vandalized and

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:57.520
looted.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.930
Chile no longer looked like the Chile it once
was.

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:04.980
Tremendously radicalized groups wanted to
end the so-called “Chilean model” with

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.080
a revolution.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.520
Of course, as you can imagine, the police
didn’t tiptoe around this issue, they responded

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.560
swiftly, and with an iron fist.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.270
That’s one thing on which the major human
rights organizations agree:

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:21.020
Reports ranging from the United Nations High
Commissioner for Human Rights to organizations

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:25.380
such as Human Rights Watch, have exposed a
collection of evidence which proves that in

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:33.290
many cases the Chilean security forces, the
Carabineros, used excessive force.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:36.790
According to data from the Ministry of Health:
the emergency services cared for more than

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:39.760
11,500 people as a result of the demonstrations.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:44.270
Of those casualties, 1,100 – so far – presented
moderate to severe injuries.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:49.380
And that’s not all, there have been more
than 350 complaints about ill-treatment, torture

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:51.080
and abuse of detainees.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:56.630
(AUDIO: “Factors such as the indiscriminate
and improper use of riot guns and shotguns;

00:03:56.630 --> 00:04:01.430
abuses against detainees and poor internal
control systems facilitated serious rights

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:03.500
violations for many Chileans.

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.480
José Miguel Vivanco, director of the Americas
for Human Rights Watch)

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:13.340
(AUDIO: Protests in Chile: UN report reiterates
that there has been a “high number” of

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:14.870
serious human rights violations.

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:15.870
BBC)

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:22.019
In any case, just to be clear, there are two
very important details we have to consider:

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:27.780
First, we do not aim to condone acts of violence.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.150
Not at all, quite the opposite!

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:34.190
These radical, violent, aggressive groups
that deserve all our condemnation had no reservations

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:38.160
when they attacked the police with stones,
benches and anything they could get their

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:39.160
hands on.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.290
Even Molotov cocktails.

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:43.400
Not to mention the looting, theft, intimidation,
etc.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:48.021
(AUDIO: “They have also been victims of
these acts of violence, in fact today, we

00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:53.690
have more than 2,500 injured police officers,
some of them quite seriously” Sebastián

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:55.130
Piñera, President of Chile)

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:59.100
On the other hand, most protesters behaved
peacefully and many suffered the consequences

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.370
of a police force that was simply not prepared
for what came.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:06.530
(AUDIO: Chile: Urgent call for police reform
after protests

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:11.800
Excessive use of force against protesters
and bystanders; serious abuses during detention.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:12.800
Human Rights Watch)

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.270
Because don’t think that the protests in
Chile were the work of a few radicals, not

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:19.060
at all.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.620
October 25th will go down in Chile’s history.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:27.220
Eleven days after the riots broke out, according
to official statistics, 1.2 million people

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:31.810
took to the streets to protest against the
country's situation and to show their discontent

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:33.600
with the path the country was taking.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:38.900
The crowd equated to more than 20% of Santiago’s
population!

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:44.990
So at this point we need to ask ourselves,
are protests of this kind really justified

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:47.100
in Latin America’s most prosperous country?

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:51.030
Well, I can already tell you that it’s a
very touchy subject.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:57.229
[“Chile has a health heritage that has cost
us a lot to conquer and which we need to look

00:05:57.229 --> 00:05:58.229
after rigorously.

00:05:58.229 --> 00:06:02.680
It allows us to be a safe and healthy food
provider for the rest of the world.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:11.880
We have free trade agreements with the world’s
main economies.]

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:18.060
(HOW CHILE HAS CHANGED IN 30 YEARS)

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.790
Let's start with the positives.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.919
“It's not 30 pesos, it's 30 years.”

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:28.639
This has been one of the main protest slogans
that, little by little, have been justified

00:06:28.639 --> 00:06:33.110
by the high inequality that Chileans have
suffered as a result of the system that has

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:36.210
prevailed in the last three decades.

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:41.040
But the question is, has this period really
been so bad?

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.669
Well, the truth is, no.

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:50.030
For example, take a look at how poverty has
changed:

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:52.660
(CHART)

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:57.430
Chile has evolved from being one of the poorest
countries in Latin America to fighting for

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:00.700
the number one spot amongst the richest and
most developed.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:05.300
In fact, if we check out the human development
index, which in addition to economic variables

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:12.770
includes things such as health care, education
and life expectancy, Chile is top in the region.

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:16.000
(CHART)

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.400
Regarding inequality, Chile truly has a lot
of inequality, but it isn’t true that it’s

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:25.930
the worst off in this matter in Latin America,
not at all.

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:26.990
(CHART)

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:34.160
What’s more, it’s one of the few countries
in the region where inequality has decreased

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:35.832
the most in recent years.

00:07:35.832 --> 00:07:41.320
This isn’t just my opinion or VisualPolitik’s,
this is coming straight from the data.

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:45.660
Because if instead of looking at inequality
overall, we focus on the inequality levels

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:50.210
between people of the same age, we’ll see
much lower rates of inequality among the younger

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:51.210
generations.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:57.770
This also happens with education.

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:01.810
If we look at the whole country, we’ll find
that in Chile the percentage of the population

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:04.889
with secondary education is lower than the
OECD average.

00:08:04.889 --> 00:08:05.889
But...

00:08:05.889 --> 00:08:09.940
If we take a look at Chileans aged between
25 and 34, we’ll see that they are already

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:14.650
surpassing the average of the richest countries.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:20.650
And this isn’t unusual, it’s a very common
feature in countries that have grown very

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:21.650
rapidly.

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:25.530
It’s easy to find very different characteristics
within a single population based on the generation.

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:30.380
One part will have grown up and lived in a
poor country, and the next in a developed

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:32.210
country.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:38.610
But perhaps the best way to approach this
mess is that taken by Rodrigo Valdés, Finance

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:41.010
Minister in Michelle Bachelet’s second term.

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:42.890
We’re not talking about a radical libertarian.

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:47.390
Check this out, in 2018 this man presented
an article at the Harvard Kennedy School where

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:49.430
he stated two things:

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:55.870
First, that the left was making mistakes left
and right by focusing so much on the issue

00:08:55.870 --> 00:09:01.700
of inequality because to combat it, economic
growth was much more important than redistribution.

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:08.490
And second, that in the last 30 years, those
who’d seen their income increase the most

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:11.570
were precisely those who had previously been
the poorest.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:14.250
Check this out:

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.690
(CHART)

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:20.290
In other words, what the country needed above
all was more economic growth.

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:23.980
And that growth has been somewhat to blame
for Chile’s current problems.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:26.940
Because as I said, it’s an extremely touchy
situation.

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:30.820
So do Chileans really have reasons to complain?

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:34.390
Listen up.

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:41.440
(THE ROOT OF DISAPPOINTMENT)

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:45.930
The fact that many things have gone well doesn’t
mean that Chileans have no reason to feel

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:46.930
disappointed.

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:48.870
Disappointed, that's the word for it.

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:51.620
Chileans were promised results that never
eventuated.

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:54.760
Despite everything we’ve seen some things
aren’t…

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:58.030
Well, so positive.

00:09:58.030 --> 00:09:59.030
For example...

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:02.590
The country has a very low productivity level
that continues to hamper wages.

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:07.850
More than 60% of the entire middle-class population
live on the edge, they are vulnerable to losing

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:09.930
that level, for even the smallest reason.

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:14.089
And the economy, another of Chile’s great
points of pride in the past, has practically

00:10:14.089 --> 00:10:16.670
stagnated, at least not in per capita terms.

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:20.120
Performance in recent years has been quite
poor.

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:25.490
And, of course, if there’s no economic growth
and real wages don’t increase...

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:27.910
Well, things don’t look so rosy, right?

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:31.970
And not just that, retirement pensions – another
of the great promises Chileans were made – are

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:32.970
very low.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:37.430
To give you an idea, half of the pensions
paid in recent months amounted to only 123

00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:46.040
thousand pesos, that’s about $160 a month.

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:49.320
(CHART)

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:50.320
Quite a miserable amount!

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:55.050
And that’s not to say that Chile's capitalization
pension system is bad, not at all.

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:59.010
What’s happening is that citizens don’t
really know how it works, because it has never

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:00.540
been properly explained to them.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:03.870
On top of that the government hasn’t been
paying close enough attention.

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:10.010
You see, on the one hand they didn’t monitor
whether the total contributions over a working

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:13.779
life were adequate, and on the other hand
they never bothered to establish a minimum

00:11:13.779 --> 00:11:16.959
safety net for those who wouldn’t manage
to contribute enough.

00:11:16.959 --> 00:11:21.321
This combination is volatile, because people
write off a reasonably good system simply

00:11:21.321 --> 00:11:25.640
because the pensions they report are very,
very low.

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:31.800
Which means the system becomes a ticking time
bomb as more and more citizens hit retirement

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:32.800
age.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:36.660
Chilean men retire at 65 with just 18 years
of contributions, on average.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:42.500
And for women it’s even worse, they retire
at 60 with an average of 13 years of contributions.

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:47.769
So, what have they been doing for the rest
of their working lives?

00:11:47.769 --> 00:11:53.269
Well… informal economy, casual work, under-the-table
agreements they didn’t declare, and so on.

00:11:53.269 --> 00:11:54.269
And let's see.

00:11:54.269 --> 00:11:58.820
How can you hope to support 20 or 30 years
of retirement if you’ve only contributed

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:02.420
10% of your salary for 13, 15 or 18 years?

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:06.540
It’s impossible, it’s impossible to be
paid higher pensions.

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:09.769
That’s why…

00:12:09.769 --> 00:12:14.040
If people don’t accurately declare their
earnings, the system simply doesn’t work,

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:16.350
not one way or another.

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:19.810
It simply doesn't work.

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:23.790
And if to all this we add the feeling that
social divisions are still very present within

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:24.790
society….

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:28.089
We can understand the enormous frustration
that fuels the protests.

00:12:28.089 --> 00:12:32.560
Although if we think about it for a second,
it seems a little unfair to focus the discontent

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:34.950
on a president who won the elections very
recently.

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:39.500
What’s more, for the almost 30 years of
Chile’s democracy, the left ruled for almost

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:41.500
25, and they are the ones protesting!

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:49.860
I mean, they must’ve had a hand in establishing
the model, don't you think?

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:52.540
Basically, expectations aren’t being met.

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:57.339
And in a way, this can be explained with what
is known as Wagner's Law, a thesis that states

00:12:57.339 --> 00:13:01.100
that the greater the wealth, the greater the
pressures for the state to provide more and

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:02.190
more social benefits.

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:05.680
And, of course, the smaller the state, the
stronger this dynamic will be.

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:13.420
But, now the question is, what’s next?

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:18.459
(THE LIGHT AT THE END OF THE TUNNEL)

00:13:18.459 --> 00:13:23.990
I think we can now understand much better
what has recently been happening in Chile.

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:25.990
The question is, will this problem end?

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:31.690
For now, President Piñera is doing two things:

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:35.640
On the one hand, he’s launching a social
agenda that, among many other issues, will

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:38.970
increase the lowest pensions and establish
a minimum living wage.

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:43.779
That is, the government will top up the payrolls
of people who work and don’t reach 350 thousand

00:13:43.779 --> 00:13:44.779
pesos.

00:13:44.779 --> 00:13:53.130
On the other hand, it has been agreed that
in 2020, Chileans will be asked if they want

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:54.709
to change their Constitution.

00:13:54.709 --> 00:14:00.420
Even though the Chilean Constitution has already
undergone 219 changes since the democratic

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:01.420
transition occurred.

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:03.300
The last significant one happened in 2005:

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:06.320
(AUDIO: “This is a very big day for Chile.

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:07.889
We have a reason to celebrate.

00:14:07.889 --> 00:14:13.430
Today we finally have a democratic Constitution
in line with the spirit of Chile.

00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:16.630
Ricardo Lagos, President of Chile from 2000
to 2006)

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:20.110
Well, he was wrong because apparently, it’s
no longer useful.

00:14:20.110 --> 00:14:21.110
What can I say?

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:25.150
Beyond the fact that it isn’t advisable
to change the constitution often or generate

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:30.149
false expectations, what Chile needs is to
focus again on growth, only then can it truly

00:14:30.149 --> 00:14:31.920
overcome all the problems it faces.

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:37.260
Anyway, I hope that this video helped you
understand everything that has recently happened

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:38.670
in this South American country.

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:41.660
VisualPolitik will be paying close attention
to see how the events unfold.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:43.000
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

